### 4.2 Requirement Traceability Matrix
<Describe how requirements / user stories will be mapped and traced dynamically with test cases.>

Because the authoritative Jira requirement body is unavailable, requirement traceability for AD-488 must be reframed around validated operational objectives rather than unavailable business stories. Traceability will therefore map directly to the known inputs preserved from Output1 and Output2: unauthorized access handling, missing description handling, logging/reporting validation, and controlled Human-in-the-Loop pause behavior. Each objective will be represented in the test management repository as a traceable quality condition with linked test cases, automated scripts, evidence records, and release decision checkpoints. This preserves governance fidelity without inventing requirements that were never retrieved.

Operationally, Jira or the selected test management layer should contain placeholder requirement artifacts referencing the issue ID, the 401 Unauthorized condition, and the absence of a description field. Test cases will then link to those artifacts using a naming convention such as AD-488-ERR-001 through AD-488-ERR-00N. Automated suites in CI/CD will publish result metadata back to the traceability layer, including build number, environment, execution timestamp, commit SHA, and log evidence location. Pull requests associated with changes to connector logic, workflow suppression, or observability handling must include explicit linkage to the traceability IDs and must not be approved unless peer reviewers confirm the related tests and evidence are updated.

The peer-review process is especially important because the system is operating under incomplete upstream business information. Developers must document whether a code change affects authentication logic, message mapping, retry behavior, or pause-state enforcement. QA reviewers then validate that the traceability map still covers all known operational objectives and that no test falsely claims business-requirement validation. If authenticated requirement access is later restored, this matrix can be expanded to include true business stories; until then, traceability remains anchored to known fault-management objectives and documented system controls.

### 4.5 Test Data Management
#### Data Requirements
<Identify the type, volume, and data source of test data required, along with masking, scrubbing, and seeding strategies.>

The test data model for this strategy is intentionally scenario-driven and centered on failure-state coverage rather than business-domain transactions. Required data sets include: unauthorized Jira connector responses (HTTP 401), payloads with missing description fields, null description payloads, malformed issue responses, expired-token events, throttled or delayed connector responses, and valid recovery payloads used only to test controlled pipeline resume after approval. Since the objective is to validate resilience and governance behavior, the data emphasis is on protocol correctness, field completeness, metadata presence, and state-transition triggers rather than end-user business content.

Test data should be sourced from mock Jira services, API virtualization frameworks, and sanitized non-production connector configurations. Any tokens, usernames, service accounts, or identifiers must be masked or replaced with synthetic values prior to storage in regression repositories. Log validation data should also be scrubbed to ensure no real credentials are echoed into searchable logs or test artifacts. Data seeding should support repeatable execution in CI/CD, including deterministic payload IDs, reusable trace IDs, and known response timing profiles so that failure classification remains consistent across builds.

